
.meta{
  title: "ChRIS: An Overview for HARBOR"
  theme: "command-line"
  transition: zoom
  label: "rudolph@artemis: ~ {n} {spoke}"
  typography:
    baseline: presentation
  snippets:
    marker: "▸ "
  css:
    "#slide-1 h1":
        font-size: "2.6em"
    ".slide .term pre.fig":
        color: "#d4d4d4"
        line-height: "1.25"
        overflow: "visible"
        margin: "0.2em 0 0.8em 1.6em"
    ".slide .term pre.fig b":
        color: "#4ec9b0"
        font-weight: "400"
    ".slide .term pre.fig u":
        display: "inline-block"
        width: "1ch"
        text-decoration: "none"
        color: "#4ec9b0"
    ".slide .term pre.fig i":
        color: "#858585"
        font-style: "normal"
}

.slide{
  .label{rudolph@artemis: ~/harbor}
  .title{Boot}
  .body{
    .h1{ChRIS}
    .h2{An overview for the HARBOR consortium}

    .div{.class{dim} Rudolph Pienaar -- Boston Children's Hospital / Harvard Medical School}

    .term{
.typewriter{$> ./chris --overview --audience=harbor}
    }
  }
}

.slide{
  .title{TL;DR}
  .body{
    .h1{The Short Version}
    .term{
.typewriter{$> chris --tldr}
      .o{ChRIS runs .bf{containerised analyses next to the data} and records how every result came to exist.}
      .o{One rule for every app: read a directory, write a directory, exit.}
      .o{Chain those apps and the directory tree .bf{is} the provenance record.}
      .o{In HARBOR it is the workflow runtime .bf{inside each Trusted Domain}.}
    }

    .takeaway{OPENSHIFT GOVERNS THE CLUSTER. ChRIS MAKES THE SCIENCE ON IT AUDITABLE.}
  }
}

.slide{
  .id{outline}
  .title{Outline}
  .body{
    .h1{Outline}
    .term{
.typewriter{$> chris --outline}
    .nexus{.id{outline}
      .div{.jump{.target{lightness} The Unbearable Lightness of Defining}}
      .div{.jump{.target{what-is} What Is ChRIS?}}
      .div{.jump{.target{contract} The App Contract}}
      .div{.jump{.target{workflows} Workflows and Provenance}}
      .div{.jump{.target{architecture} Architecture}}
      .div{.jump{.target{clinic} ChRIS in the Clinic}}
      .div{.jump{.target{harbor} Where ChRIS Sits in HARBOR}}
      .div{.jump{.target{roadmap} What HARBOR Adds to ChRIS}}
      .div{.jump{.target{working} Working With It}}
      .div{.jump{.target{conclusions} Conclusions}}
    }
    }
  }
}

.slide{
  .id{lightness}
  .title{The Unbearable Lightness of Defining}
  .body{
    .h1{The Unbearable Lightness of Defining}
    .h2{What is an iPad? Everyone knows. Now define it.}
    .term{
.typewriter{$> whatis ipad}
    .nexus{.id{ipad-answers}
      .div{.jump{.target{mobile-os} "It's a mobile OS."}}
      .div{.jump{.target{pictures} "It's pictures you touch."}}
      .div{.jump{.target{crippled-mac} "It's a crippled Mac."}}
      .div{.jump{.target{so-chris} So what is ChRIS?}}
    }
    }
  }
}

.slide{
  .id{mobile-os}
  .title{It's a Mobile OS}
  .body{
    .h1{"It's a Mobile OS"}
    .term{
.typewriter{$> define "mobile OS"}
      .o{Fine. What is a mobile OS?}
      .o{Is it a different kind of thing from a non-mobile OS? Or the same thing in a smaller case?}
      .o{A laptop is mobile. Nobody calls macOS a mobile OS.}
      .o{The answer traded one undefined word for another.}
    }
    .takeaway{NAMING THE CATEGORY ISN'T DEFINING THE THING.}
  }
}

.slide{
  .id{pictures}
  .title{It's Pictures You Touch}
  .body{
    .h1{"It's Pictures You Touch"}
    .term{
.typewriter{$> describe --by=experience ipad}
      .o{A screen of little pictures. Each picture is an app.}
      .o{Touch one and it does a thing: reports the weather, shows the news, plays a game.}
      .o{So an iPad is little pictures that do things. So is an iPhone. So is any tablet, any phone.}
      .o{It's an honest definition. It describes what people met.}
    }
  }
}

.slide{
  .id{pictures-2}
  .title{Now Change the UI}
  .body{
    .h1{Now Change the UI}
    .term{
.typewriter{$> launcher --replace}
      .o{<pre class="fig">
 ┌──────────────────────┐            ┌──────────────────────┐
 │ [wx]   [news] [game] │            │ $ weather            │
 │ [mail] [maps] [cam]  │   ─────<u>▶</u>   │ $ news               │
 │ [web]  [pay]  [chat] │            │ $ _                  │
 └──────────────────────┘            └──────────────────────┘
  <i>pictures you touch</i>                  <i>a prompt you type at</i>
</pre>}
      .o{On Android the whole home screen can be replaced. Mine runs .bf{Re:T-UI}: no icons, no pictures, a prompt.}
      .o{Same hardware, same apps underneath.}
      .o{By the pictures definition it stopped being a phone. It didn't.}
    }
    .takeaway{PEOPLE DEFINE A THING BY HOW THEY MET IT.}
    .source{Re:T-UI launcher guide, re-tui.pages.dev}
  }
}

.slide{
  .id{crippled-mac}
  .title{It's a Crippled Mac}
  .body{
    .h1{"It's a Crippled Mac"}
    .h2{The architect's answer}
    .term{
.typewriter{$> describe --by=construction ipad}
      .o{<pre class="fig">
 <b>macOS</b>     child processes  ·  pipes  ·  one filesystem  ·  any UI you like
   │       <i>same kernel (XNU), same core (Darwin)</i>
   <u>▼</u>
 <b>iPadOS</b>    every app sandboxed  ·  no child processes  ·  a private container each
</pre>}
      .o{A computer, running a close relative of macOS.}
      .o{With an app model that fights the OS underneath it: no .bf{fork}, no pipes between apps, no shared filesystem.}
      .o{For the sake of security: a crippled macOS behind a very restrictive UI.}
      .o{This is subjective too. It's how an architect meets the thing.}
    }
    .source{Apple Platform Security, "Security of runtime process in iOS, iPadOS, and visionOS" · apple-oss-distributions/xnu · Apple Developer Forums thread 747499}
  }
}

.slide{
  .id{crippled-mac-2}
  .title{Which Definition Survives?}
  .body{
    .h1{Which Definition Survives?}
    .term{
.typewriter{$> diff --swap-ui experience construction}
      .o{Swap the launcher. "Pictures you touch" is now false.}
      .o{"Sandboxed apps, no pipes, no shared files" is still true.}
      .o{A definition by experience describes the interface.}
      .o{A definition by construction says what the thing can and can't do.}
    }
    .takeaway{KEEP THE DEFINITION THAT SURVIVES A CHANGE OF UI.}
  }
}

.slide{
  .id{so-chris}
  .title{So What Is ChRIS?}
  .body{
    .h1{So What Is ChRIS?}
    .h2{Ask five people}
    .term{
.typewriter{$> whatis chris --ask=5}
      .o{"A dashboard for running analyses." They met ChRIS_ui.}
      .o{"A full web UI with a shell. It looks like a whole computer." They met ARGUS.}
      .o{"The thing that pulls studies out of a PACS from the CLI." They met chell.}
      .o{"A persistent record of all analyses and transformations." They met a feed.}
      .o{"A workflow engine." They met a pipeline.}
      .o{All true. All pictures you touch.}
      .o{ChRIS_ui, ARGUS and chell are three surfaces. Swap one for another and it's still .bf{ChRIS}.}
    }
    .takeaway{DEFINE ChRIS THE WAY AN ARCHITECT DEFINES AN iPad.}
    .source{github.com/FNNDSC/mise}
  }
}

.slide{
  .id{so-chris-2}
  .title{The Same Move, Reversed}
  .body{
    .h1{The Same Move, Reversed}
    .term{
.typewriter{$> diff ipad chris --app-model}
      .o{<pre class="fig">
 <b>iPad</b>     [app]  │  [app]  │  [app]      <i>walls between apps: isolation</i>
 <b>ChRIS</b>    [app] ──<u>▶</u> [app] ──<u>▶</u> [app]      <i>a directory handed on: composition</i>
</pre>}
      .o{An iPad is defined by what its apps are forbidden: no pipes, no shared files.}
      .o{ChRIS is defined by a rule just as strict: read a directory, write a directory, exit.}
      .o{The iPad restricts apps to keep them apart. ChRIS restricts them so they compose.}
      .o{The iPad forbids the pipe between apps. ChRIS is the pipe.}
    }
    .takeaway{THE APP MODEL IS THE DEFINITION. THE UI IS ONE WAY TO MEET IT.}
  }
}

.slide{
  .id{what-is}
  .title{What Is ChRIS?}
  .body{
    .h1{What Is ChRIS?}
    .term{
.typewriter{$> whatis chris}
      .o{An .bf{open-source platform} for running computation on medical data, wherever that data has to stay.}
      .o{The name is recursive: .bf{Ch}RIS .bf{R}esearch .bf{I}ntegration .bf{S}ervice.}
      .o{Built at Boston Children's (FNNDSC), with Red Hat and the Mass Open Cloud.}
      .o{It runs a specific kind of program: no interaction once started, arguments on the command line, results in files.}
      .o{That covers most of research imaging -- and most of an ingestion pipeline.}
    }
    .source{FNNDSC/CHRIS_docs · Pienaar et al., IEEE EMBC 2015}
  }
}

.slide{
  .id{what-is-2}
  .title{Where It Came From}
  .body{
    .h1{Where It Came From}
    .term{
.typewriter{$> git log --oneline --reverse chris}
      .o{.span{.class{dim} pre-2010} bash scripts gluing analysis programs together.}
      .o{.span{.class{dim} 2010 v1} a web front end over those scripts and an in-house HPC.}
      .o{.span{.class{dim} 2013 v2} the ideas that stuck: .bf{feeds} and .bf{plugins}. Still HPC-bound.}
      .o{.span{.class{dim} 2017 v3} REST services and containers. Any cluster that runs Linux containers. (Called CHIPS in the papers of that year.)}
    }
    .takeaway{THE GLUE KEPT OUTGROWING THE TOOLS. SO THE GLUE BECAME THE PLATFORM.}
    .source{FNNDSC/CHRIS_docs, History · Pienaar et al., LNCS 10494, 2017 (CHIPS)}
  }
}

.slide{
  .id{contract}
  .title{The App Contract}
  .body{
    .h1{The App Contract}
    .term{
.typewriter{$> app [--flags] /input /output}
      .o{<pre class="fig">
   /input              ┌───────────────────────┐              /output
 ┌─────────┐           │ <b>plugin</b>: one container │           ┌─────────┐
 │  files  │ ────────<u>▶</u> │ app --flags           │ ────────<u>▶</u> │  files  │
 └─────────┘           │ runs once, then exits │           └─────────┘
                       └───────────────────────┘
</pre>}
      .o{A ChRIS app -- a .bf{plugin} -- is a Linux container that reads one directory, writes another, and exits.}
      .o{It does not know about ChRIS, the workflow it sits in, or the cluster under it.}
      .o{A JSON descriptor declares its parameters and its limits: CPU, memory, GPU.}
      .o{Python, R, shell, compiled code, a call out to a model service. The boundary doesn't change.}
    }
    .takeaway{IF IT RUNS IN A CONTAINER AND WRITES FILES, IT CAN BE A PLUGIN.}
    .source{ChRIS Plugin Specification, FNNDSC/CHRIS_docs (specs/ChRIS_Plugins.adoc)}
  }
}

.slide{
  .id{contract-2}
  .title{Three Kinds of Plugin}
  .body{
    .h1{Three Kinds of Plugin}
    .term{
.typewriter{$> chris plugin --types}
      .o{<pre class="fig">
 <b>fs</b>                     <b>ds</b>                          <b>ts</b>
 [app] ──<u>▶</u> /out         /in ──<u>▶</u> [app] ──<u>▶</u> /out      /in ─┐
                                                    /in ─┼<u>▶</u> [app] ──<u>▶</u> /out
                                                    /in ─┘
</pre>}
      .o{.bf{fs} -- feed synthesis. No input directory. Brings data in: a PACS pull, an upload, a download.}
      .o{.bf{ds} -- data synthesis. Input in, output out. Nearly everything is one of these.}
      .o{.bf{ts} -- topology synthesis. Reshapes the graph itself, joining branches into one node.}
      .o{De-identification, format conversion, segmentation, embedding, a training step: all .bf{ds}.}
    }
    .source{ChRIS Plugin Specification, "Plugin Type"}
  }
}

.slide{
  .id{contract-end}
  .title{Wrap Before Inventing}
  .body{
    .h1{Wrap Before Inventing}
    .term{
.typewriter{$> cat plugin-doctrine.txt}
      .o{ChRIS doesn't need to own every anonymiser, validator, harmoniser or model runner.}
      .o{A good external tool gets a container and a declared interface. That's usually the whole job.}
      .o{What it gains: explicit I/O, recorded parameters, a pinned version, a place in a workflow.}
      .o{Write a plugin from scratch only when nothing adequate exists, or the plugin is the research.}
    }
    .takeaway{NOT "DID WE WRITE IT?" BUT "DOES IT OBEY THE CONTRACT?"}
  }
}

.slide{
  .id{workflows}
  .title{Workflows and Provenance}
  .body{
    .h1{Workflows and Provenance}
    .term{
.typewriter{$> tree feed/}
      .o{<pre class="fig">
 <b>feed</b>
 └─ pacs-pull              <i>fs   brings the study in</i>
    └─ dicom-normalise     <i>ds</i>
       └─ de-identify      <i>ds</i>
          ├─ segment       <i>ds</i>
          │  └─ measure    <i>ds</i>
          └─ embed         <i>ds   every node keeps its own output directory</i>
</pre>}
      .o{A workflow is a tree of plugin runs. One node's output directory is the next node's input.}
      .o{HARBOR's documents call this the .bf{filesystem-bus}.}
      .o{Every intermediate output stays where it was written, and stays inspectable.}
      .o{A .bf{feed} is one such tree, run on one dataset. A .bf{pipeline} is the reusable template for it.}
    }
    .takeaway{THE UNIX PIPE, STRETCHED ACROSS A CLUSTER.}
  }
}

.slide{
  .id{workflows-2}
  .title{Data State, Not Control Flow}
  .body{
    .h1{Data State, Not Control Flow}
    .term{
.typewriter{$> diff control-flow data-state}
      .o{<pre class="fig">
 <b>control flow</b>    [A] ──ok?──<u>▶</u> [B] ──ok?──<u>▶</u> [C]
                 <i>a controller decides what runs next</i>

 <b>data state</b>      /d0 ──A──<u>▶</u> /d1 ──B──<u>▶</u> /d2 ──C──<u>▶</u> /d3
                 <i>every arrow leaves a directory behind</i>
</pre>}
      .o{Control flow is Argo and Tekton. Data state is Make, and ChRIS.}
      .o{A ChRIS node is finished when its output is on disk. Not when a controller says so.}
      .o{Gating, branching and joining are plugins in the graph -- so they are in the record too.}
      .o{The cost: patterns that are one line in Argo take an explicit step here.}
    }
    .takeaway{WHAT RAN, IN WHAT ORDER AND WHY: READ IT OFF THE DATA.}
  }
}

.slide{
  .id{workflows-end}
  .title{The Five Questions}
  .body{
    .h1{The Five Questions}
    .term{
.typewriter{$> provenance --explain result.dcm}
      .o{What data went in?}
      .o{Which app ran, and which version of it?}
      .o{With what parameters?}
      .o{What came out?}
      .o{How did this final result come to exist?}
      .o{Answered by walking a directory tree. No log archaeology, no unordered database queries.}
    }
    .takeaway{MAKE THE HANDOFFS BORING. MAKE THE DATA STATE EXPLICIT.}
  }
}

.slide{
  .id{architecture}
  .title{Architecture}
  .body{
    .h1{Architecture}
    .term{
.typewriter{$> chris --components}
      .o{<pre class="fig">
 ┌──────────┐  REST  ┌──────────────────────────────┐  DICOM  ┌────────┐
 │ ChRIS_ui │ ─────<u>▶</u> │             <b>CUBE</b>             │ <u>◀</u>────── │  PACS  │
 │   mise   │        │ users files feeds provenance │  <i>pfdcm</i>  └────────┘
 └──────────┘        └───────┬──────────────┬───────┘ <i>oxidicom</i>
                             │              │
                         ┌───┴───┐      ┌───┴───┐
                         │ pfcon │      │ pfcon │   <i>one per</i>
                         └───┬───┘      └───┬───┘   <i>compute environment</i>
                         OpenShift         HPC
</pre>}
      .o{.bf{CUBE} -- users, files, plugins, feeds, permissions, provenance. One REST API.}
      .o{.bf{pfcon} -- runs a plugin as a job on Docker, Kubernetes, OpenShift, or SLURM.}
      .o{.bf{pfdcm, oxidicom} -- the PACS bridge: query, retrieve, receive, register into CUBE.}
    }
    .source{FNNDSC/CHRIS_docs · FNNDSC/pfcon · FNNDSC/oxidicom · FNNDSC/mise}
  }
}

.slide{
  .id{architecture-end}
  .title{Who Owns What}
  .body{
    .h1{Who Owns What}
    .term{
.typewriter{$> chris --layers}
      .o{<pre class="fig">
 <b>CUBE</b>       meaning      which app, which directory, which parameters
   <u>▼</u>
 <b>pfcon</b>      mapping      that run, on this backend
   <u>▼</u>
 <b>cluster</b>    execution    pods, storage, policy
</pre>}
      .o{.bf{CUBE owns meaning. pfcon materialises it. The cluster executes.}}
      .o{One CUBE can drive several compute environments: a workstation, an HPC, a cloud.}
      .o{The lock-in test: can the same workflow still run somewhere without Kubernetes?}
    }
    .takeaway{REPLACE MECHANISMS FREELY. DON'T REPLACE THE MEANING OF THE WORKFLOW.}
  }
}

.slide{
  .id{architecture-posix}
  .title{ChRIS as a Computer}
  .body{
    .h1{ChRIS as a Computer}
    .h2{mise: drive ChRIS like a computer, not a web API}
    .term{
.typewriter{$> chell}
      .o{<pre class="fig">
 <b>cd</b> /home/chris/uploads/SAG-anon                <i>data is a filesystem</i>
 <b>ls</b> /bin                                        <i>plugins are executables</i>
 <b>pl-fshack-v1.2.0</b> --inputFile brain.mgz         <i>run an analysis by name</i>
 <b>cat</b> /proc/jobs/feed_123/pl-fshack_789/status   <i>a running job is a file</i>
</pre>}
      .o{One useful task used to be a long chain of dependent REST calls.}
      .o{.bf{mise} builds that plumbing once. Nothing above it touches the raw API.}
    }
    .takeaway{A MACHINE INSTEAD OF AN ENDPOINT.}
    .source{github.com/FNNDSC/mise}
  }
}

.slide{
  .id{architecture-posix-2}
  .title{Above CUBE}
  .body{
    .h1{Above CUBE}
    .term{
.typewriter{$> mise --layers}
      .o{<pre class="fig">
 <b>chell  argus</b>   how you look       a terminal, a browser
   <u>▲</u>
 <b>calypso</b>        who is there       one login, one live session
   <u>▲</u>
 <b>mise</b>           where things are   /home  /bin  /proc  /SERVICES/PACS
   <u>▲</u>
 <b>CUBE</b>           what is true       the objects and the laws between them
</pre>}
      .o{Two views, never two copies: type .bf{cd} in the terminal and the browser has already moved.}
      .o{Below CUBE you can swap the cluster. Above it you can swap the surface.}
    }
    .takeaway{CUBE SAYS WHAT IS TRUE. mise SAYS WHERE THINGS ARE.}
    .source{github.com/FNNDSC/mise}
  }
}

.slide{
  .id{clinic}
  .title{ChRIS in the Clinic}
  .body{
    .h1{ChRIS in the Clinic}
    .h2{Leg-length discrepancy at Boston Children's}
    .term{
.typewriter{$> chris feed --from=pacs --run=lld}
      .o{<pre class="fig">
 PACS ──<u>▶</u> [pull] ──<u>▶</u> [LLD model] ──<u>▶</u> [measure] ──<u>▶</u> [DICOM report] ──<u>▶</u> PACS ──<u>▶</u> reading room
          └───────────────────<i> one ChRIS feed </i>──────────────────┘
</pre>}
      .o{A study lands from PACS. A workflow runs on it. The measurement goes back as DICOM, into the tools radiologists already use.}
      .o{The model is a Docker container. The same one that ran on the research bench.}
      .o{Validated prospectively: a .bf{shadow trial} first, then a .bf{clinical trial}.}
      .o{No bespoke integration per algorithm. The next model reuses the plumbing.}
    }
    .takeaway{SAME CONTRACT, FROM THE BENCH TO THE READING ROOM.}
    .source{Tsai, Samal, Lamonica, Morris, McNeil, Pienaar, Eur Radiol 36:1698-1708 (online Sep 2025), doi:10.1007/s00330-025-12022-0}
  }
}

.slide{
  .id{clinic-end}
  .title{Where It Has Run}
  .body{
    .h1{Where It Has Run}
    .term{
.typewriter{$> chris --deployments}
      .o{A laptop: the whole stack in docker-compose (miniChRIS).}
      .o{On-prem at Boston Children's, wired to clinical PACS.}
      .o{The Mass Open Cloud, on OpenShift, with GPUs.}
      .o{Across institutions: FreeSurfer in the cloud feeding a multi-party computation, so two hospitals compare brain volumes without either seeing the other's data.}
    }
    .takeaway{THE CONTAINER DOESN'T CHANGE. ONLY WHERE IT RUNS.}
    .source{FNNDSC/miniChRIS-docker · FNNDSC/CHRIS_docs · Grant, Red Hat Summit 2019 (ChRIS and multi-party compute)}
  }
}

.slide{
  .id{harbor}
  .title{Where ChRIS Sits in HARBOR}
  .body{
    .h1{Where ChRIS Sits in HARBOR}
    .term{
.typewriter{$> harbor td --describe}
      .o{<pre class="fig">
 ┌─────────────────────────── Hub TD ───────────────────────────┐
 │      app catalogue  ·  coordination  ·  aggregation          │
 └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
       <u>▼</u> <i>apps and instructions</i>            <u>▲</u> <i>approved outputs only</i>
 ┌────────────────────────── Spoke TD ──────────────────────────┐
 │  <b>ChRIS</b>:  [app] ──<u>▶</u> [app] ──<u>▶</u> [app]      raw data stays here  │
 │  OpenShift: namespaces, policy, storage                      │
 └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>}
      .o{A TD is the airport. Apps are aircraft: machinery flies in, no raw data flies out.}
      .o{.bf{ChRIS is how that machinery runs once it has landed}: the workflow runtime inside every TD.}
      .o{What may leave is HARBOR policy. ChRIS supplies the record that policy is checked against.}
    }
    .takeaway{THE SAME WORKFLOW, UNDER THE SAME CHECKS, IN EVERY TD.}
    .source{HARBOR TDD, TA2 Technical Architecture; Major Task 2.6}
  }
}

.slide{
  .id{harbor-2}
  .title{The Boundary}
  .body{
    .h1{The Boundary}
    .h2{What ChRIS owns in a Trusted Domain, and what it leaves alone}
    .term{
.typewriter{$> chris --scope}
      .o{.bf{Owns:} the app contract, the plugin lifecycle, workflow composition, provenance, and inspection of what ran.}
      .o{.bf{Doesn't own:} OpenShift provisioning, fleet management, enterprise identity, cluster policy, SOC 2 and HIPAA operations.}
      .o{What ChRIS hands the platform teams: service manifests, deployment expectations, health checks, validation tests.}
    }
    .takeaway{ChRIS ISN'T COMPETING WITH OPENSHIFT. IT IS THE LAST MILE ON TOP OF IT.}
  }
}

.slide{
  .id{harbor-end}
  .title{Ingestion as Plugins}
  .body{
    .h1{Ingestion as Plugins}
    .h2{Major Task 2.7: every stage of intake is a ChRIS app}
    .term{
.typewriter{$> harbor ingest --stages}
      .o{<pre class="fig">
 raw ──<u>▶</u> [validate] ──<u>▶</u> [de-identify] ──<u>▶</u> [deduplicate] ──<u>▶</u> [normalise] ──<u>▶</u> <b>promoted</b>
             │ <i>fail</i>
             <u>▼</u>
     [quarantine + report]
</pre>}
      .o{DICOM standardisation, tag normalisation, and a metadata validation gate.}
      .o{De-identification: headers, text, pixels.}
      .o{Embeddings (MedSigLIP) for deduplication. Conversion to the at-rest formats.}
      .o{Built and tested one plugin at a time, then composed into one workflow and proven on .bf{three health systems}.}
    }
    .takeaway{EACH PARTNER'S STAGE IS A CONTAINER WITH A DIRECTORY ON EITHER SIDE.}
    .source{HARBOR TDD, Major Task 2.7 (Data Ingestion and Processing)}
  }
}

.slide{
  .id{roadmap}
  .title{What HARBOR Adds to ChRIS}
  .body{
    .h1{What HARBOR Adds to ChRIS}
    .term{
.typewriter{$> chris --gaps --scale=federation}
      .o{<pre class="fig">
 <b>Airflow</b>     schedule · retry · monitor · policy hooks
    │ <i>run</i>                      <u>▲</u> <i>events: start, progress, done, error</i>
    <u>▼</u>                          │
 <b>ChRIS</b>       [app] ──<u>▶</u> [app] ──<u>▶</u> [app] ──<u>▶</u> provenance ──<u>▶</u> OpenLineage
    │
 <b>OpenShift</b>   pods · storage · isolation
</pre>}
      .o{Today's runtime is .bf{synchronous}. Federation means thousands of concurrent nodes.}
      .o{So: async execution, non-blocking I/O, faster filesystem-bus throughput.}
      .o{Events pushed to dashboards and audit, not polled.}
      .o{Airflow schedules and retries. The plugin stays the unit of work.}
    }
    .takeaway{THE CONTRACT IS PROVEN. THE THROUGHPUT AT FEDERATION SCALE IS THE FUNDED WORK.}
    .source{HARBOR TDD, Major Task 2.6 (Data Pipeline Orchestration and Provenance)}
  }
}

.slide{
  .id{roadmap-end}
  .title{Governance and Standards}
  .body{
    .h1{Governance and Standards}
    .h2{Making the record legible outside ChRIS}
    .term{
.typewriter{$> chris --extensions --for=harbor}
      .o{.bf{Policy hooks} at the orchestration layer: data use agreements and compute quotas, checked on every run.}
      .o{.bf{Audit metadata} captured on every pipeline execution.}
      .o{.bf{OpenLineage} events from the filesystem-bus, so any lineage service can read ChRIS provenance.}
      .o{.bf{DICOMweb} endpoints inside the TD: QIDO-RS to query, WADO-RS to retrieve, STOW-RS to store.}
    }
    .takeaway{PROVENANCE AN AUDITOR CAN READ WITHOUT LEARNING ChRIS.}
    .source{HARBOR TDD, Major Task 2.6}
  }
}

.slide{
  .id{working}
  .title{Working With It}
  .body{
    .h1{Working With It}
    .h2{Bringing a tool into a HARBOR workflow}
    .term{
.typewriter{$> chris plugin --howto}
      .o{Put the tool in a container.}
      .o{Give it the contract: flags, an input directory, an output directory. No prompts, no GUI.}
      .o{Describe its parameters and resource needs in the JSON descriptor.}
      .o{Register it with a CUBE. Now it can be a node in anyone's workflow.}
      .o{Try the whole stack on a laptop first: miniChRIS, in docker-compose.}
    }
    .source{ChRIS Plugin Specification · github.com/FNNDSC/miniChRIS-docker · chrisproject.org}
  }
}

.slide{
  .id{working-end}
  .title{What We Need to Agree}
  .body{
    .h1{What We Need to Agree}
    .term{
.typewriter{$> harbor --interfaces --open}
      .o{.bf{Stage contracts.} What exactly sits in the directory between your stage and the next one.}
      .o{.bf{Policy hook points.} Where in a run the TD checks a data use agreement or a quota.}
      .o{.bf{Telemetry schema.} The events a job publishes, and who consumes them.}
      .o{.bf{Lineage mapping.} How a ChRIS run becomes OpenLineage run, job and dataset facets.}
    }
    .takeaway{AGREE THE DIRECTORIES AND THE EVENTS. THE REST CAN CHANGE UNDERNEATH.}
  }
}

.slide{
  .id{conclusions}
  .title{Conclusions}
  .body{
    .h1{Conclusions}
    .term{
.typewriter{$> exit
> questions?}
      .o{ChRIS is a .bf{workflow runtime}: containers that read a directory and write a directory, chained.}
      .o{The provenance isn't bolted on. It is the directory tree the workflow leaves behind.}
      .o{It already runs clinical AI at Boston Children's, on the same contract.}
      .o{In HARBOR it is the layer inside each TD that makes local execution repeatable and auditable.}
      .o{HARBOR's work on it is scale, telemetry and standards. The contract stays.}
    }
  }
}
